package "main"

// eval.funcvalue.deref-call: calling a func value obtained by dereferencing a pointer,
// `(*p)()`, must LOAD the func value from `*p` and issue an INDIRECT call through it --
// exactly like the deref-to-local desugaring `var g @func() int = *p; g()` (which works).
//
// It does NOT today: codegen mis-lowers the fused `(*p)()` into a DIRECT call to a bogus,
// never-defined mangled symbol (`@bn_..._main0_`) instead of an indirect call, so clang
// aborts (`use of undefined value`).  The call TARGET is miscomputed (not merely
// mis-linked): reproduces for managed `@func` and raw `*func`, capturing and non-
// capturing, with/without args, even for a NAMED top-level function bound to the pointer.
// Controls that work: bind-then-call (`var g = *p; g()`), `getf()()`, `arr[0]()`, `h.f()`.
//
// PRE-EXISTING and orthogonal to the named-distinct-pointer -> interface work (needs no
// box / interface / named-distinct type).  XFAIL until the fused `(*p)()` lowering loads
// the func value from `*p` and calls indirectly.  Tracked in explorations/claude-todo.md
// (MAJOR: (*p)() func-value-deref-call miscompiles).

func plain() int {
	return 42
}

func main() {
	var f @func() int = plain
	var p *(@func() int) = &f
	println((*p)())
}
